import LeanCertifyTest.Fixpoint

/-! Cost of the table check (`LeanCertify/Fixpoint.lean`), on tables the
compiled producers of `LeanCertifyTest/Fixpoint.lean` made (this file is
generated). Each theorem is one kernel check of a whole table.

Measured 2026-10-21, Lean v4.31.0, Apple silicon, `lean -DElab.async=false
-Dprofiler=true` ("type checking" is the kernel check of the theorem):

| table | entries | kernel |
|---|---|---|
| `collatz 27` | 112 | 26 ms |
| `collatz 77031` | 351 | 97 ms |
| `ack (3, 3)` | 154 | 58 ms |
| `ack (3, 4)` | 315 | 134 ms |
| `ack (3, 5)` | 636 | 303 ms |
| `worstF`, 101 | 101 | 30 ms |
| `worstF`, 201 | 201 | 68 ms |
| `worstF`, 401 | 401 | 194 ms |

Elaborating the table literal costs about as much again. `worstF` is the
worst case for `lookup` (every entry reads the oldest one, which `lookup`
finds last): doubling the table multiplies the time by about 2.9, the
quadratic term showing. In the producers' post-order a call usually reads
recent entries, which `lookup` finds first. A few hundred entries cost a
fraction of a second; a keyed structure would only matter far beyond. -/

open Lean.Order Certify Certify.Fixpoint

/-- The worst case for the lookups: every call also reads the oldest entry,
which `lookup` finds last. -/
def worstF (rec : Nat → Option Nat) (n : Nat) : Option Nat :=
  if n = 0 then some 0 else do
    let z ← rec 0
    let r ← rec (n - 1)
    some (r + z + 1)

theorem worstF_mono : monotone worstF := by
  unfold worstF
  repeat monotonicity

def worstCert := certifier worstF worstF_mono

